    "1:1–5 — 'The former treatise have I made, O Theophilus, of all that Jesus began both to do and teach.' Began. Luke's Gospel was not the end of the doing. This book is what he continues to do, now from heaven, by the Spirit, through witnesses. Forty days of appearing, speaking of the kingdom of God. A charge: do not leave Jerusalem, but wait for the promise of the Father. 'John truly baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence.' Luke 24:49 already named that promise. Acts opens by refusing to start the mission on yesterday's power.",
    "1:6–8 — They ask the question Israel has been trained to ask: 'Lord, wilt thou at this time restore again the kingdom to Israel?' He does not say the hope is a mistake. He says the timetable is not theirs: 'It is not for you to know the times or the seasons, which the Father hath put in his own power.' That sentence is Daniel 2:21 on different lips — the same claim Matthew already heard in the first volume's world. Then the map that structures the whole book: 'Ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.' The kingdom question is not mocked. It is postponed as a clock and opened as a mission.",
