    "1:12–14 — Olivet to the upper room, a Sabbath day's journey. The Eleven are named. The women, Mary the mother of Jesus, and his brothers — the brothers who once stood outside in Mark 3 and Matthew 12 are now in the prayer meeting. 'These all continued with one accord in prayer and supplication.' The church's first work, before a single sermon in this book, is waiting together.",
    "1:15–20 — About a hundred and twenty. Peter stands and treats Scripture as the next actor: the Holy Ghost spoke by the mouth of David concerning Judas. Two psalms, named as fulfilled: 'Let his habitation be desolate' (Psalm 69:25) and 'His bishoprick let another take' (Psalm 109:8). Judas bought a field with the reward of iniquity, fell, burst asunder. Matthew 27 told the priests buying the potter's field and Judas hanging. Acts tells the field and the fall. Two cameras on one death. Do not spend the hour flattening the mechanics. Spend it on Peter's instinct: the empty office is a Scripture problem, not only a personnel problem.",
    "1:21–26 — The twelfth must be a man who companied with them from John's baptism to the ascension, a witness of the resurrection. Two names: Joseph called Barsabas, and Matthias. They pray: 'Thou, Lord, which knowest the hearts of all men, shew whether of these two thou hast chosen.' Lots. Matthias is numbered with the eleven. Proverbs 16:33 sits under the lot — the lot is cast, the disposal is the Lord's. Paul will later call himself an apostle untimely born (1 Corinthians 15:8). This paragraph is not trying to settle Paul's later office. It is trying to put twelve back in place before Pentecost, because Israel-witness still has twelve names."
